<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=control-of-fire">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Control of fire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The control of fire by early humans gave warmth, light, protection from predators and a means of cooking; the earliest known controlled use of fire took place in Wonderwerk Cave, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, 1.79–1.0 million years ago. Heated fish teeth at the Daughters of Jacob Bridge in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golan-heights">Golan Heights</a>, about 780,000 years ago, are the oldest likely evidence of cooking. The oldest definitive evidence of fire-making, about 400,000 years ago, comes from a Neanderthal site in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where burnt soil was found with flint handaxes and iron pyrite used to strike sparks.</p></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 1 million years ago</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Wonderwerk Cave, South Africa</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Claims reach back 1.5 to 2 million years, but traces of wood ash from about 1 million years ago are the evidence with the firmest scholarly support.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">Stone tools<span>3.3 million years ago</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/pottery">Pottery<span>c. 18,000 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/copper-smelting">Copper smelting<span>c. 5000 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=control-of-fire">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
